  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nIn the `GaugeManager.sol` contract, there is a function `createGauge`. This function allows anyone to push a pool into the `pools` array.\n\nThis function validates that the `_pool` we intend to add has some code and is not an EOA:\n```solidity\nrequire(_pool.code.length > 0, \"CODELEN\");\n```\nHowever, the function does not validate that the pool is not malicious.\nAs a result, an attacker can simply deploy a malicious pool which reverts in its `collectFees` function.\n\nWhen the function `distributeFees(uint256,uint256)` is later called, it iterates through some pools depending on the input params.\n```solidity\n   function distributeFees(uint256 _start, uint256 _finish) external nonReentrant {\n        for (uint256 x = _start; x < _finish; x++) {\n            address _pool = pools[x];\n            _distributeFees(_pool);\n        }\n    }\n```\nHowever, because the attacker added a malicious pool that reverts in its `collectFees` function, the tx will always revert. Ideally for the attacker, if the first pool in the `pools` array is the malicious pool, all txs will revert, no matter the input params.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nTo mitigate this issue, do not allow anyone to push pools of random code into the `pools` array.\nOr deploy the pools using create2 opcode and cloning a specific pool (CLPool in this case).",
